    "core": 58
  },
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "num": "01",
      "en": "Here's a {riddle} : What do {traffic jams} , long {lines} and {waiting} for a {vacation} to start all have in {common} ? There is one answer.They're all {situations} where we could use a little {extra patience} .",
      "cn": "有这样一个 谜题 ： 交通堵塞 、排长 队 和 等待假期 开始，这三者有什么 共同 点？答案只有一个。在这些 情况 下，我们都需要 多 一点 耐心 。"
    },
    {
      "num": "02",
      "en": "In the {Digital} Age, we're used to having what we need {immediately} and right at our {fingertips} .However, research suggests that if we practiced patience, we'd be a {whole} lot better off.Just as a {sculpture} takes time to shape, only those who wait long enough can reveal the {essence} of life.Here are several {tricks} .",
      "cn": "在这个 数字 时代，我们习惯于动动 手指 就能 马上 获得我们需要的一切。然而，研究表明，如果我们能培养耐心，我们的生活就会好很多。正如 雕塑 需要时间来成形一样，只有那些等待时间足够长的人才能揭示生活的 真谛 。这里有几个 诀窍 。"
    },
    {
      "num": "03",
      "en": "Maintain a {tidy} space",
      "cn": "杂乱的空间会导致思维混乱，在压力大的情况下很难让人保持冷静。例如，匆忙穿过繁忙的 机场 之前，你应该整理好你的 钱包 ，确保所有重要文件，如 现金 、 护照 和身份 证 都井然有序。"
    },
    {
      "num": "04",
      "en": "A messy space leads to a confused mind, making it difficult to remain calm during stressful situations.For instance, before rushing through a busy {airport} , you should organize your {wallet} and make sure all the important documents such as your {cash} , {passport} and ID {card} are in order.",
      "cn": "每天留出时间，也许是 中午 ，也许是晚上，点一根 蜡烛 ，专注于你的呼吸。这也有助于我们 培养 耐心，并对生活心怀 感激 。这种做法可以像你度过了愉快的时光时对 主人 说“ 谢谢 ”一样简单。研究表明，感恩让我们更快乐、压力更小，甚至更 乐观 。"
    },
    {
      "num": "05",
      "en": "Take time to contemplate ( (沉思) ) and practice {gratitude}",
      "cn": "即时 满足在当时看来可能是最“感觉良好”的 选择 ，但 心理学 研究表明，从长远来看，等待 实际上 会让我们更快乐。从小的 任务 开始。把最喜欢的节目推迟 到周末 再看，或者享用 蛋糕 之前多等上10分钟。在等待迟到的朋友时，利用这个机会阅读一本 小册子 或坐 公共汽车 去附近的商店，把耽搁的时间变成富有成效的时间。你很快就会发现，你培养的耐心越多，你就越能 开始 把它 应用 到其他更 烦人的场合 。"
    },
    {
      "num": "06",
      "en": "Setting aside time each day, perhaps during {noon} or in the evening, to light a {candle} and focus on your breath.It can also help us {foster} patience and become {thankful} for our lives.This practice can be as simple as saying“ {thank} you” to the {host} when you have a good time, for example.Research shows that thankfulness makes us happier, less stressed and even more {optimistic} .",
      "cn": "我们有许多人都 相信 ， 舒适 是我们唯一能 容忍 的 状态 ，而当我们经历 舒适区 之外的事情时，我们就会对这类情况感到 不耐烦 。你应该学会对自己说：“这 只是 令人有些不舒服，并非 无法忍受 。”这样你就会 逐渐 变得更 有耐心 。 MKqjwFu4arM4cgPc+LiB66EBg/AaE0u6XBr6IpfU5EiRRdX6Ad+uTD+Kdly4W5UG"
    },
    {
      "num": "07",
      "en": "Make yourself wait",
      "cn": ""
    },
    {
      "num": "08",
      "en": "{Instant} gratification ( (满足) ) may seem like the most “feel good” {option} at the time, but {psychology} research suggests waiting for things {actually} makes us happier in the long run.Start with small {tasks} .Put off watching your favorite show {until} the {weekend} or wait 10 extra minutes before going for that {cake} .When waiting for a friend who is running late, use the opportunity to read a {brochure} or take a {bus} to nearby shops, turning the delay into a productive time.You'll soon find that the more patience you practice, the more you {start} to {apply} it to other, more {annoying occasions} .",
      "cn": ""
    },
    {
      "num": "09",
      "en": "{Accept} the {uncomfortable}",
      "cn": ""
    },
    {
      "num": "10",
      "en": "So many of us have the {belief} that being {comfortable} is the only {state} we will {tolerate} , and when we experience something outside of our {comfort zone} , we get {impatient} about the circumstances.You should learn to say to yourself,“This is {merely} uncomfortable, not {intolerable} .”You'll then {gradually} become more {patient} .",
      "cn": ""
    }
  ],
